2,\n    \"mass\": 0.48895708719889325,\n    \"marginal\": 0.034895833333333334,\n    \"group\": 0.04807495801761996\n  },\n  \"bonferroni\": {\n    \"utility\": 0.8488598331486145,\n    \"mass\": 0.5079944398668077,\n    \"marginal\": 0.025477430555555562,\n    \"group\": 0.03890005447026749\n  }\n}\n[\n  \"actual\",\n  \"bonferroni\",\n  \"constant\",\n  \"global_ilr\",\n  \"permuted\",\n  \"raw_linf\"\n]\n{\"wall_clock_limit_seconds\": 1200, \"elapsed_seconds\": 129.02530720698996, \"max_rpc_calls\": 1375, \"rpc_calls\": 117, \"outcome\": \"failed\", \"objective_counts\": \"advisory\"}# Noise-aware latent composition coverage: interface assessment\n\nAttempt: `c01_noise_aware_latent_composition_bridge_attempt_01`\n\nIdea: `c01_noise_aware_latent_composition_bridge`\n\nCandidate: `program_1`\n\n## Implemented scope\n\nThis candidate preserves the starter's numerical scale estimator, RPC responses,\narray representation, and transfer/state protocol. The source docstring records\nthe incompatibility below. The proposed noise enlargement and research audit are\n**not implemented**. No unused bridge helpers, fabricated observations, additional\nRPC operations, or extra response fields are introduced.\n\n## Why the proposed method cannot be expressed\n\nFit supplies six-dimensional training covariates, scalar training residual norms,\nthe composition dimension, and scale bounds. Prediction receives only covariates.\nThe reset seed does not identify a workload. The candidate controls positive\nscalar scales, not centers, geometry, or calibration.\n\n| Proposed component | Missing interface capability |\n| --- | --- |\n| Corrected conformal order statistic | Calibration scores, sample size, miscoverage level, and control of calibration |\n| Multinomial noise radius | Announced integer count depth and a stated multinomial observation model |\n| Additive enlargement of an L1 radius | Compatible geometry, calibrated radius, and region-construction control |\n| Observed-versus-latent coverage audit | Observed compositions, latent vectors, prediction centers, and test outcomes |\n| Exact score-atom experiment | Controlled count generation, centers, and independent calibration repetitions |\n| Paired seed-level bootstrap | Actual repeated experimental outcomes for both targets |\n\nThe dimension alone cannot identify a multinomial noise radius. No meaning for\nindividual covariate columns is documented, so none is interpreted as depth, a\nlatent target, or a noise parameter. Residual norms do not identify the sampling\nmodel or separate observation noise from prediction error.\n\nAn output scale cannot generally encode an additive radius enlargement without\nthe unknown calibrated radius and compatible geometry. Changing training scales\nis therefore not claimed to implement the bridge. Generating separate synthetic\ndata inside the learner would neither supply the missing workload observations\nnor validate latent coverage for this interface.\n\n## Conditional mathematical proposal, not an experimental result\n\nIf an interface explicitly supplies compositions Y=C/M under conditional\nmultinomial sampling, known depth M, and L1 regions, the proposed argument is:\n\n    ||Y-p||_1 = 2 max_A sum_{j in A}(Y_j-p_j).\n\nOne-sided Hoeffding bounds over the 2^D-2 nonempty proper subsets give\n\n    P(||Y-p||_1 > epsilon) <= (2^D-2) exp(-M epsilon^2/2).\n\nFor 0 < delta < alpha < 1, the proposed noise radius is\n\n    epsilon = min(2, sqrt(2 log((2^D-2)/delta)/M)).\n\nUnder joint exchangeability conditional on an independently trained center,\nobserved-target calibration at miscoverage alpha-delta gives L1 radius q. The\ncorrected calibration rank is ceil((n_cal+1)(1-alpha+delta)); a rank beyond n_cal\nrequires an infinite radius. Inclusive comparisons retain ties. The triangle\ninequality and union bound then yield marginal latent coverage at least 1-alpha\nfor radius min(2,q+epsilon). Clipping at two uses the simplex's L1 diameter.\nIndependence of the error events is unnecessary; covariate-conditional coverage\ndoes not follow.\n\nFor the proposed two-component boundary-center counterexample, the zero-score\natom has probability (1-epsilon_rare)^M. A zero calibration quantile requires at\nleast ceil((n_cal+1)(1-alpha)) zero calibration scores. Its probability is a\nbinomial upper tail when that rank is feasible, and zero otherwise. With interior\ncenter (a,1-a), where 0<a<1/(2M), the unique lowest-score atom instead has score\n2a and the same probability. If epsilon_rare>2a, its radius excludes the latent\nvector. These are analytical observations, not measured frequencies.\n\nThese arguments do not justify use with unknown depths, overdispersed counts,\narbitrary softmax outputs, covariate shift, or latent probabilities reconstructed\nfrom single categorical labels. They have not been implemented or empirically\nchecked in this candidate.\n\n## Execution and evidence status\n\nOnly permitted filesystem inspection, copying, source/documentation editing,\nand append-only memory updates were performed. No candidate code, tests,\nexperiments, evaluation, package installation, or network access was executed.\nNo literature was accessed and no forbidden material was encountered. There are\nno measured coverage results, uncertainty intervals, performance improvements,\npaper/PDF, or completed experimental claims from this attempt. The proposed\nexperimental grid remains unexecuted.\n\nThe `apply_patch` command was unavailable; the initial patch attempt did not\nmodify source files. Plain filesystem editing was used instead.\n\n`program_spec.toml` and the numerical learner remain unchanged. The existing\n`program.py` entrypoint continues to implement `python_rpc_v1:handle`. Only the\nharness's later official evaluation can establish candidate performance.\n# Cross-fitted tail-adaptive residual scaling\n\n## Scope and interface\n\n`program.py:handle` retains the starter's fit/predict/reset and bounded-transfer\nprotocol. Only supplied training covariates and training residual norms enter\nlearning. Fit still returns only `{\"ok\": true}`; predict still returns only the\nencoded float64 `scales` array. Calibration, composition centers, geometry, and\nregion construction remain evaluator-owned. No extra observations are assumed.\n\n## Prespecified learner\n\n- The incumbent remains quadratic-feature ridge regression of log residual norm,\n  with fold-local centering, standardization, the original ridge penalties, and\n  original log-prediction bounds. Its original residual floor is retained.\n- The tail learner targets the 0.9 quantile of\n  `log(max(residual_norm, 1e-12))`. This is a surrogate learning target, not an\n  assumed conformal miscoverage level.\n- Each stump uses training-only thresholds at 15 equally spaced quantile levels\n  from 0.05 to 0.95 for each supplied feature. Both leaves require at least\n  `max(20, ceil(0.03 * fitting_sample_size))` observations. Updates are within-leaf\n  empirical residual quantiles. The learning rate is 0.1; at most 80 rounds are\n  fitted, stopping when no candidate reduces training pinball loss beyond a\n  numerical tolerance. Constant features and undersized folds yield an\n  intercept-only tail predictor. Repeated residual values are allowed.\n- To avoid repeatedly sorting every leaf, each round sorts working residuals\n  once. Cumulative membership counts recover interpolated leaf quantiles; exact\n  pinball losses of the shrunken updates choose the split. The selected leaf\n  updates are then recomputed with NumPy's empirical quantile operation.\n- Three deterministic folds use `np.random.default_rng(0).permutation(n)` and\n  `np.array_split`. Each fold fits its own incumbent preprocessing, tail\n  thresholds, and quantile alignment offsets using only fitting observations.\n- Six candidates, in fixed simplicity order, are incumbent, constant, and\n  log-space incumbent/tail blends with tail weights 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, and 1.0.\n  Every candidate receives a fitting-only additive log offset equal to the\n  empirical 0.9 quantile of target minus fitted log scale. Aligning a blend this\n  way is unchanged by first applying constant offsets to its component models.\n- Candidate losses are mean validation pinball losses within each fold. Their\n  unweighted fold means are compared; the standard error is the sample standard\n  deviation across the three fold losses divided by sqrt(3). The first candidate\n  within one standard error of the lowest mean loss is selected. This is a\n  regularization heuristic, not a significance test or a confidence interval:\n  fitting folds overlap. For fewer than three training observations the\n  incumbent is used without cross-validation. Empty training arrays are rejected.\n- Models and offsets are refitted using all training observations. Prediction is\n  read-only and does not depend on query-batch statistics. Selected log scales\n  are bounded to [-30, 30] before exponentiation, with a positive numerical floor.\n  The documented evaluator additionally clips scales to [0.0001, 10000].\n\nNo aggregate evaluation feedback enters any choice. Reset seeds retain their\nprotocol meaning and do not select workloads or hyperparameters. Selection\nstatistics are internal and are not added to RPC responses.\n\n## Validity argument and limitations\n\nCondition on the training data and all fitting choices. The learned scale is\nthen a fixed strictly positive function. If calibration and test observations\nare exchangeable conditional on those choices, and use the same evaluator-owned\ncenter, geometry, and numerical scale clipping, their normalized residual scores\nare exchangeable. For m calibration scores and k = ceil((m+1)(1-alpha)), the usual\nsplit-conformal threshold at rank k (infinity if k > m) gives marginal coverage\nat least 1-alpha, with ties conservative. Training-only selection does not change\nthis rank argument. It does not imply conditional/group coverage, nor validity\nunder distribution shift or calibration reuse. The argument presumes the\nevaluator actually uses this split-conformal construction; the candidate does\nnot inspect or alter it.\n\nPinball loss on log norms is not simplex volume or the harness objective. The\nselection rule may favor an inferior efficiency tradeoff. Additive stumps miss\ninteractions; irrelevant features, small samples, finite threshold grids, and\ntail sparsity can impair learning. Numerical clipping can limit adaptivity and\nbreak otherwise irrelevant global-scale invariance. No improvement, runtime,\ncoverage, or uncertainty estimate for the harness metrics has been measured.\n\n## Execution status\n\nThis stage permits source edits and static filesystem checks only. No candidate,\ntests, benchmark, evaluator, package manager, network request, or exploratory\ncomputation was executed. Positivity, batch-invariance, degeneracy, and protocol\ntests remain unperformed; their expected behavior is supported only by source\nreasoning, not test evidence. The official evaluation is harness-owned.\n\nThis is a candidate implementation, not a completed research paper. Repeated-run\nexperiments, numerical uncertainty quantification, literature review, an official\nconference-template manuscript/PDF and checklist, measured dependency versions,\nand measured resource use remain incomplete. No fabricated results, citations,\nor paper claims are supplied. No prohibited literature was accessed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
