\"d443f6a41e158e87ab147e7fe339bc9bbca302da7d734e7fe3def306eac48649\",\n      \"26d04317d1f40578bd14f5c0be02700e79142ff87fc1709dde84844412f66a19\",\n      \"beee2f8bdb32b886d6e61b2166a87700932b2335a78d6244e63a2c49b693eafc\",\n      \"83abd71381fd320513200b15747c49de8b4aea30888023d8f1d87187a2febb6e\",\n      \"a35586f50312fb00c9b233380e81defe060ae0919fcf30846f55fa607cf8c20d\",\n      \"cc0d97e4766321df8f3ed8f59cfcaf11f278242a8089de5617c211439246f5c8\",\n      \"9bdf0d27fce9642fc244982adc60cca1a0a1fdf560cd9824ad223456dc904531\",\n      \"a531996a955eba46b3c9a37bc81cfc0965bd254612c2eb4d29262b7be05a0b58\",\n      \"301e2f7c6e8c0797b57c7a6546df1d08fd7fa779c0027b0a694e34b244724a17\",\n      \"fa17365b86482fa462a889f7ea5f3c8b785da68893c7920be1c3349aff8f5d27\",\n      \"9e783a0bbd430c493f138ad3a1bc1dfe9f595ad33f85d96b5da07047fd02b77e\",\n      \"4685a3a6dcb5ccb2e6d8351a5dd6708a607cba3336e1544e11afa2a9e7f5fa94\",\n      \"e929bab03fa9b6abaf11fe7ebfd60682128d1d3905bf6078340cd6de495cf76c\",\n      \"96e0b333a04d2e5006036bead77f28cff310478575295742e2faabc88f8236a7\",\n      \"8bf8497187f158b6ca4d081bdd899aaddbe2215b7381a1dcb45ff1b9869a924b\",\n      \"ba2fcbc72cc55eeecc324919ee0f58df3e4d89ad6406573505e2af1e31e3ac7d\",\n      \"182ef53de040fda936912aa8c049e254361ef96da77329960a44e10cbbf46a27\",\n      \"86523fbf306f4e40d0a8008bb618d5df7039e8b75408f9418fd335091b549646\",\n      \"eb48d52b6ed733c8695c0f635998418ba786fc3a955c7f92ea9b8f98c3f34adf\",\n      \"78d5013fcd1dd7b4e4fdf4771fcd9b39d6a6501e17c8cb3f6c55506211bc1095\",\n      \"ae321036be25458cfc8b76e8e499a1617cfe36e3a24d035bdd8aaf054bd25e72\",\n      \"5a1409fc1aeb5e5d182ade20bfef534c5f95082497a45db979f9ccfb5a4649f1\",\n      \"d21ba74d77174661a28dae82450310840347fb1a358706ac16fabaec7411e629\",\n      \"55cf342a9cf9a11c8da876a3caaf883c8e7cbc65f1eb0c57c31f6df856a85c5c\",\n      \"4dda743ea9cd6b5e8a8ba48534671b3059414744b0069b8d58f8357e00f9133b\",\n      \"f734efd75a55b672bc588ea3218eab56ff1bd40460cbeb09485b40ebd13c4519\",\n      \"20fe3dfa1d8e862c7a3a50aeeba4161a112633a9259a7bcfedabe3c9a6778cc2\",\n      \"612c3fa69e630609e8558d551680f83264f34b122ae84f34528ed9d2138de7f7\",\n      \"ba129d22d07052266182ab25366c5df12d0395e69949633faa5d7e3bdbe0fa6f\",\n      \"e65bf5d265e2b902da713561cecc0b93bd48eb4c2fff55f9364a22c586af0265\",\n      \"2bb99d5f3a5f3af09319c9a953c6e0e687ede7881fca99d63f712a690526ffac\",\n      \"024fe8ca79b2098b2014460f6b0b3fc559bb86d26a4189c7710b9430e0714c46\",\n      \"62c0cc60c1dbbbadf4f26f594a7a79e91fe07273b50122a483be075943b702d7\",\n      \"eca31c91c440c5204a2b2ee0347981b13aa00013ada40dc89d0b622dc026866c\",\n      \"4ab270c15442a795c9f8b79b9a45ac01edf2b987eb4e6bdcf90d1e4003d4268c\",\n      \"2919603243efbcd9560e53b88b668b2eaae3381127e717e2e49efa07ec4f5b76\",\n      \"bea27cb3e1b9b87503668196388d8fcaaa9feaab0f0b3c8a96113d8cc3880771\",\n      \"b30cd9362763dfc4523ec734dde1fabae71d8f1e66da31ec26a2706eb2249fe4\",\n      \"e68761a03e7d86149c4fdbfc0d88adb5cb2001ac9a4b862eb0889646fcdab7f6\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"keys\": [\n    \"aggregation\",\n    \"canonicalization\",\n    \"controls\",\n    \"cross_plane_check\",\n    \"environment\",\n    \"files\",\n    \"generator_description\",\n    \"generator_license\",\n    \"limitations\",\n    \"normalization\",\n    \"operation_table\",\n    \"production_seeds\",\n    \"schema_version\",\n    \"smoke_seeds\"\n  ]\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 18,\n  \"claim\": \"Only measured final observations enter numerical comparisons. Missing measurements from unsuccessful extensions are not assigned a low measured score. The available qualification record states that the final assessment completed and met its declared checks; unspecified gate diagnostics remain unknown. Qualification is not evidence of external validity or component attribution.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 19,\n  \"claim\": \"\\\\section{Results}\\n\\\\subsection{Aggregate Comparison With Matched Baselines}\\nTable~\\\\ref{tab:aggregate} summarizes the final evaluation. Local scaling attains mean utility 0.87723 with seed-bootstrap interval [0.87662, 0.87813]. Its paired difference from global ILR is 0.04762, with interval [0.04390, 0.05062]. The mean utility differences from raw $L_\\\\infty$ and Bonferroni are 0.03058 and 0.02837, with paired intervals [0.02831, 0.03268] and [0.02600, 0.03121], respectively. These intervals describe the aggregate of the evaluated conditions; they do not imply an advantage in each condition or on an unseen dataset.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 20,\n  \"claim\": \"\\\\begin{table}[t]\\n\\\\centering\\n\\\\caption{Equal-weight final means across seeds and conditions. Lower errors and mass are preferable; higher utility is preferable. Reference mass is not volume. Paired utility intervals are reported in the text.}\\n\\\\label{tab:aggregate}\\n\\\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\\n\\\\toprule\\nMethod & $U$ & $E_m$ & $E_g$ & $M$\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\midrule\\nLocal scale & 0.87723 & 0.01173 & 0.01511 & 0.45084\\\\\\\\\\nGlobal ILR & 0.82960 & 0.05737 & 0.06878 & 0.49235\\\\\\\\\\nRaw $L_\\\\infty$ & 0.84665 & 0.03490 & 0.04807 & 0.48896\\\\\\\\\\nBonferroni & 0.84886 & 0.02548 & 0.03890 & 0.50799\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bottomrule\\n\\\\end{tabular}\\n\\n\\n\\\\end{table}\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 21,\n  \"claim\": \"\\\\subsection{Homoscedastic and Heteroscedastic Conditions}\\nFigure~\\\\ref{fig:coverage} shows that the homoscedastic conditions provide a meaningful negative control. Mean local and global utilities are 0.85319 versus 0.85326 for H3, and 0.87887 versus 0.88028 for H10. Their mean reference masses are nearly equal: 0.55883 versus 0.55803 and 0.45106 versus 0.45105. Thus there is no consistent homoscedastic advantage to offset the complexity of learning a scale. We do not interpret these small observed differences as a precisely established disadvantage; no condition-specific inferential interval is supplied.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 22,\n  \"claim\": \"\\\\begin{figure}[t]\\n\\\\centering\\n\\\\includegraphics[width=\\\\textwidth]{figures/coverage_by_condition.pdf}\\n\\\\caption{Joint coverage by condition, dimension, and nominal level. Small dots show individual seeds; horizontal colored marks show means; dashed lines show nominal coverage. H: homoscedastic; B: boundary-concentrated heteroscedastic; S: B response mechanism with shifted test covariates. Panels share an axis spanning all observations. Shifted coverage is an empirical stress test, not a validity guarantee.}\\n\\\\label{fig:coverage}\\n\\n\\\\end{figure}\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 23,\n  \"claim\": \"In B3 and B10, local utility rises to 0.89045 and 0.90820, compared with global utilities 0.84703 and 0.84162. The contrast is not merely a pooled score effect: fixed-group error decreases from 0.07770 to 0.01816 in B3 and from 0.07181 to 0.01723 in B10. Reference mass also decreases, from 0.47580 to 0.39349 and from 0.49497 to 0.32211. Local mean coverage at nominal 0.90 is 0.89689 in B3 and 0.90706 in B10. These joint observations support improved allocation under the evaluated heteroscedastic mechanism, while the remaining discrepancies preclude claiming exact conditional calibration.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 24,\n  \"claim\": \"Raw-coordinate methods are useful alternatives rather than uniformly weak baselines. Bonferroni is conservative in these heteroscedastic conditions: at nominal 0.90 its mean coverage is 0.93465 in B3 and 0.93717 in B10. Its group errors are lower than global ILR's but higher than the local procedure's. Raw $L_\\\\infty$ also improves some global-ILR summaries without matching the local procedure's aggregate tradeoff. The comparison concerns complete region constructions. It cannot separate coordinate geometry, calibration multiplicity, and learned adaptation into isolated causal contributions.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 25,\n  \"claim\": \"\\\\subsection{Shift Stress Test and the Cost of Coverage Repair}\\nThe shift exposes a substantial gap between global calibration and local scaling in the recorded data. At nominal 0.90, global ILR covers 0.74748 of responses in S3 and 0.70190 in S10; local scaling covers 0.89933 and 0.91610. At nominal 0.95 the corresponding global means are 0.85067 and 0.81405, versus local means 0.94979 and 0.95768. The raw-coordinate baselines mitigate some of this undercoverage but do not reach the same mean coverage in these conditions. This is evidence about the specified covariate shift, not evidence that ordinary split conformal becomes valid under arbitrary shift.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 26,\n  \"claim\": \"\\\\begin{figure}[t]\\n\\\\centering\\n\\\\includegraphics[width=\\\\textwidth]{figures/reference_mass_by_condition.pdf}\\n\\\\caption{Reference mass, averaged over the two nominal levels within each seed. Dots show seeds and marks show means under the shared mixed reference measure. The S3 local region has greater mass than global ILR; improved coverage must not be summarized as uniformly smaller regions. These values are not intrinsic simplex volumes.}\\n\\\\label{fig:mass}\\n\\n\\\\end{figure}\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 27,\n  \"claim\": \"Figure~\\\\ref{fig:mass} makes the efficiency qualification explicit. S3 local mass is 0.50795, exceeding global ILR's 0.47945. A global region that misses many shifted responses can look artificially economical. Local scaling pays additional mass while restoring empirical coverage in that condition. S10 instead has local mass 0.47159 versus global mass 0.49482. The two outcomes rule out a uniform region-size claim even within this limited evaluation. They also illustrate why mass and coverage should be inspected together rather than collapsing the interpretation into one scalar.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 28,\n  \"claim\": \"\\\\subsection{Alignment Controls and Attribution Limits}\\nThe constant-covariate control agrees with global ILR, with aggregate utility 0.82960. Permuting training residual targets gives utility 0.82985, compared with 0.87723 for aligned local scaling; the paired interval for the aligned-minus-permuted utility difference is [0.04313, 0.05109]. This comparison supports the relevance of covariate--residual alignment to the complete procedure under this design. Permutation also changes model selection and estimation, so it does not isolate a particular functional component. All declared control checks pass in the final record, which is a diagnostic result rather than a separate scientific guarantee.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 29,\n  \"claim\": \"The empirical scope is narrow: continuous, strictly positive synthetic compositions at two dimensions and two nominal levels. Exact zeros, sparse count responses, real topic proportions, and estimated class-probability vectors are not evaluated. A prediction region for the observed continuous response is not automatically a confidence region for a latent composition. Nor do the results compare label-set classification methods. Six seeds quantify repeatability only coarsely; the bootstrap cannot recover unobserved distributional diversity or correct selection biases outside the frozen final assessment.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 30,\n  \"claim\": \"The four partition sizes, in center/scale/calibration/test order, are $(1024,1024,512,2048)$. The S shift applies only to the test partition. The stored raw-generation manifest identifies the example lineages and disjointness checks; these identifiers establish provenance, not extra measurements. The split-conformal theorem uses independence of fitting from calibration and exchangeability of calibration with a new response; it does not require that every partition have equal size.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 31,\n  \"claim\": \"The ridge log target uses a residual floor of $10^{-8}$, while the tail target uses $10^{-12}$. Standard deviations for covariate standardization are floored at $10^{-8}$. The ridge intercept penalty is $10^{-8}$ rather than exactly zero. Prediction clips ridge log scales before blending and clips the final exponent argument to $[-30,30]$ before applying a positive floor. The evaluator then applies its common scale clipping. These are numerical details, not evidence that saturation never occurs; no saturation diagnostic is recorded.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 32,\n  \"claim\": \"\\\\begin{table}[h]\\n\\\\centering\\n\\\\caption{Condition-specific mean utility $\\\\pm$ sample standard deviation over seeds. Comparisons are matched within each seed; these marginal standard deviations are not paired difference intervals.}\\n\\\\label{tab:case}\\n\\\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\\n\\\\toprule\\nCondition & Local & Global ILR & Raw $L_\\\\infty$ & Bonferroni\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\midrule\\nH3 & $0.8532\\\\pm0.0046$ & $0.8533\\\\pm0.0046$ & $0.8455\\\\pm0.0035$ & $0.8422\\\\pm0.0063$\\\\\\\\\\nH10 & $0.8789\\\\pm0.0047$ & $0.8803\\\\pm0.0045$ & $0.8655\\\\pm0.0049$ & $0.8601\\\\pm0.0075$\\\\\\\\\\nB3 & $0.8904\\\\pm0.0049$ & $0.8470\\\\pm0.0038$ & $0.8564\\\\pm0.0035$ & $0.8519\\\\pm0.0033$\\\\\\\\\\nB10 & $0.9082\\\\pm0.0058$ & $0.8416\\\\pm0.0128$ & $0.8593\\\\pm0.0022$ & $0.8530\\\\pm0.0053$\\\\\\\\\\nS3 & $0.8604\\\\pm0.0047$ & $0.7924\\\\pm0.0154$ & $0.8204\\\\pm0.0139$ & $0.8445\\\\pm0.0110$\\\\\\\\\\nS10 & $0.8722\\\\pm0.0044$ & $0.7631\\\\pm0.0210$ & $0.8327\\\\pm0.0075$ & $0.8415\\\\pm0.0090$\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bottomrule\\n\\\\end{tabular}\\n\\n\\n\\\\end{table}\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 33,\n  \"claim\": \"\\\\begin{table}[h]\\n\\\\centering\\n\\\\caption{Mean fixed-group absolute error and reference mass for local and global ILR regions, averaging both nominal levels within each seed.}\\n\\\\label{tab:group}\\n\\\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\\n\\\\toprule\\nCondition & $E_g$ local & $E_g$ global & $M$ local & $M$ global\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\midrule\\nH3 & 0.01035 & 0.01087 & 0.55883 & 0.55803\\\\\\\\\\nH10 & 0.01137 & 0.00929 & 0.45106 & 0.45105\\\\\\\\\\nB3 & 0.01816 & 0.07770 & 0.39349 & 0.47580\\\\\\\\\\nB10 & 0.01723 & 0.07181 & 0.32211 & 0.49497\\\\\\\\\\nS3 & 0.01906 & 0.10816 & 0.50795 & 0.47945\\\\\\\\\\nS10 & 0.01446 & 0.13488 & 0.47159 & 0.49482\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\bottomrule\\n\\\\end{tabular}\\n\\n\\\\end{table}\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 34,\n  \"claim\": \"The stored paired utility interval against the uncalibrated comparator is [0.04434, 0.04862]. This comparator changes both the sample used to choose a radius and the quantile convention. It therefore does not isolate the numerical finite-sample correction. The comparator's role is a diagnostic for replacing held-out calibration with a training-residual threshold, not a clean ablation of the rank correction alone.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 35,\n  \"claim\": \"The full-simplex control has coverage and reference mass equal to one throughout the final records. The singleton-center control has observed coverage and reference mass equal to zero in those records. Those are measured membership outcomes in this continuous-response evaluation, not assumed properties to be substituted for measurements. Geometry checks also verify corrected-quantile behavior, including an infinite threshold when the required rank exceeds calibration size. Reset/replay checks test reproducibility of the scale interface, not distribution-free validity.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 36,\n  \"claim\": \"\\\\subsection{Reproducibility, Source Linkage, and Evaluation Scope}\\nThe recorded evaluation environment is Linux x86\\\\_64, CPython 3.12, and NumPy 2.1.3, using CPU execution and single-threaded BLAS. These are recorded evaluator settings, not a claim that the present manuscript-building environment is identical. The anonymous reproduction instructions in \\\\texttt{REPRODUCIBILITY.md} identify dependencies, seeds, commands, source locations, and figure/table generation. A portable copy of the evaluator, selected learner, and frozen raw resources is supplied under \\\\texttt{reproduction/}; its scientific invocation was not rerun during writing. All plotted numerical data come from the frozen final records in \\\\texttt{paper\\\\_data.json}.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 37,\n  \"claim\": \"Two development directions do not provide extra measured evidence for the thesis: a count-noise/latent-target extension was not expressible through the scale-only interface and was not implemented; an interaction-tree extension has an unsuccessful evaluation with no measured score and no identified failure cause. Neither is a measured loss or a successful ablation. We do not infer whether the latter failed statistically, operationally, or because of a validity restriction. Development scores are not compared numerically with final scores, and the absence of a matched final ridge-scale baseline remains a limitation.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 38,\n  \"claim\": \"The final qualification sources record completion and a qualified outcome, with the declared checks satisfied. No failed final qualification is present in the supplied registry. This status does not certify conditional validity, shift validity, or an explanation of component effects. Missing gate diagnostics are left unknown. The stored elapsed durations include evaluator work and are not reported as isolated learner runtime. New scientific experiments, model refits, and added performance measurements were not run during manuscript preparation.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 39,\n  \"claim\": \"The complete selected procedure, not an isolated stump component, is the object supported by the final comparison.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 40,\n  \"claim\": \"Search scores are not final results; the original learned ridge scale has no matched repeated-final comparator; missing component diagnostics prevent causal attribution.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 41,\n  \"claim\": \"No failed final qualification is recorded; unspecified gate diagnostics remain unknown.\"\n}\n{\n  \"index\": 42,\n  \"claim\": \"Every supplementary utility and group/mass table entry is an arithmetic mean or sample standard deviation of the matched final seed records.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
