e conformal predictors\t\t\t\n\tConformal prediction beyond exchangeability\t\t\t\n\tQuantile Regression Forests\t\t\t\n\\paragraph{Conformal prediction and adaptation.}\nThe conformal framework and its inductive formulation establish the calibration perspective used here \\citep{Vovk2005,Papadopoulos2002}. Distribution-free regression and prediction-band constructions clarify the distinction between coverage guarantees and statistical efficiency \\citep{Lei2018,LeiWasserman2014}. Locally adaptive scores and conformalized quantile regression motivate adapting uncertainty to heteroscedasticity \\citep{AngelopoulosBates2021,Romano2019}. Our procedure predicts a scalar residual-norm scale; it does not fit lower and upper response quantiles as in conformalized quantile regression. Nor does it implement localized calibration weights \\citep{Guan2023} or leave-one-out refitting such as jackknife+ \\citep{Barber2021}. These methods provide context, not additional evaluated baselines.\n\n\\paragraph{Composition and conditional scale estimation.}\nLogistic-normal distributions provide a continuous model on the interior simplex \\citep{AitchisonShen1980}; log-ratio analysis avoids treating constrained coordinates as unconstrained measurements \\citep{Aitchison1982}. The isometric transform supplies Euclidean coordinates for relative geometry \\citep{Egozcue2003}. Within that representation, our scale family combines ridge regularization \\citep{HoerlKennard1970} with quantile-loss fitting \\citep{KoenkerBassett1978,KoenkerHallock2001} and shallow additive updates motivated by boosting \\citep{Friedman2001,Friedman2002}. Quantile regression forests are a different conditional-quantile approach \\citep{Meinshausen2006}; we do not evaluate forests. Cross-validation and conservative model selection are standard tools rather than novel contributions \\citep{Stone1974,Hastie2009}.\n\n\\paragraph{What reliability can mean.}\nConditional-validity distinctions matter even when marginal calibration is theoretically justified \\citep{Vovk2012,BarberLimits2021}. We therefore report fixed-group discrepancies without claiming distribution-free conditional coverage. Weighted conformal methods under covariate shift require additional structure \\citep{Tibshirani2019}; robustness beyond exchangeability is a separate methodological problem \\citep{BarberBeyond2023}. We implement neither correction. Finally, Wilson intervals and bootstrap summaries quantify different sources of empirical uncertainty \\citep{Wilson1927,Efron1979}: the former describe individual coverage proportions, whereas our main comparative interval resamples independent seeds. No literature comparison is used as a substitute for a measured experimental comparison.\n0: \\begin{abstract} Predictive regions for compositions must describe uncertainty jointly over a vector constrain\n1: \\section{Introduction} Compositions represent proportions of a whole: each coordinate is nonnegative and the c\n2: Split-conformal prediction offers a useful separation of responsibilities: a fitted model proposes a score, an\n3: \\section{Related Work} \\paragraph{Conformal prediction and adaptation.} The conformal framework and its induct\n4: \\paragraph{Composition and conditional scale estimation.} Logistic-normal distributions provide a continuous m\n5: \\paragraph{What reliability can mean.} Conditional-validity distinctions matter even when marginal calibration\n6: \\section{Method} \\subsection{Problem Setup and Joint Regions} Let $X\\in\\R^p$ and let $Y$ lie in the interior s\n7: For a calibration set of size $n_c$, write \\begin{equation}  R_i=\\frac{\\|Z_i-\\widehat m(X_i)\\|_2}{\\widehat s(X\n8: The inspected learner fits $T_i=\\log\\max(r_i,10^{-12})$, where $r_i$ is a training residual norm. Its referenc\n9: Candidate log scales are the ridge prediction, a constant, and ridge--stump blends with stump weights 0.25, 0.\n10: Exponentiation produces positive scales. The evaluator applies the same clipping interval, $[10^{-4},10^4]$, a\n11: Diagnostic controls address narrower questions. A constant-covariate control must agree with global ILR calibr\n12: \\subsection{Validity and Its Boundaries} Condition on the independent samples used to fit the center and scale\n13: \\section{Experimental Protocol} \\subsection{Synthetic Conditions and Information Separation} The frozen genera\n14: Each condition uses 1,024 center-training, 1,024 scale-training, 512 calibration, and 2,048 test observations.\n15: Reference mass is a probe-based size measure, not intrinsic simplex volume. At 128 fixed test-covariate locati\n16: The predeclared utility combines these summaries: \\begin{equation}  U=1-\\left[0.75\\left(\\tfrac12 E_m+\\tfrac12 \n17: \\subsection{Uncertainty and Reporting Policy} The stored evaluation supplies approximate 95\\\n18: Only measured final observations enter numerical comparisons. Missing measurements from unsuccessful extension\n19: \\section{Results} \\subsection{Aggregate Comparison With Matched Baselines} Table~\\ref{tab:aggregate} summarize\n20: \\begin{table}[t] \\centering \\caption{Equal-weight final means across seeds and conditions. Lower errors and ma\n21: \\subsection{Homoscedastic and Heteroscedastic Conditions} Figure~\\ref{fig:coverage} shows that the homoscedast\n22: \\begin{figure}[t] \\centering \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/coverage_by_condition.pdf} \\caption{Jo\n23: In B3 and B10, local utility rises to 0.89045 and 0.90820, compared with global utilities 0.84703 and 0.84162.\n24: Raw-coordinate methods are useful alternatives rather than uniformly weak baselines. Bonferroni is conservativ\n25: \\subsection{Shift Stress Test and the Cost of Coverage Repair} The shift exposes a substantial gap between glo\n26: \\begin{figure}[t] \\centering \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/reference_mass_by_condition.pdf} \\capt\n27: Figure~\\ref{fig:mass} makes the efficiency qualification explicit. S3 local mass is 0.50795, exceeding global \n28: \\subsection{Alignment Controls and Attribution Limits} The constant-covariate control agrees with global ILR, \n29: The empirical scope is narrow: continuous, strictly positive synthetic compositions at two dimensions and two \n30: The four partition sizes, in center/scale/calibration/test order, are $(1024,1024,512,2048)$. The S shift appl\n31: The ridge log target uses a residual floor of $10^{-8}$, while the tail target uses $10^{-12}$. Standard devia\n32: \\begin{table}[h] \\centering \\caption{Condition-specific mean utility $\\pm$ sample standard deviation over seed\n33: \\begin{table}[h] \\centering \\caption{Mean fixed-group absolute error and reference mass for local and global I\n34: The stored paired utility interval against the uncalibrated comparator is [0.04434, 0.04862]. This comparator \n35: The full-simplex control has coverage and reference mass equal to one throughout the final records. The single\n36: \\subsection{Reproducibility, Source Linkage, and Evaluation Scope} The recorded evaluation environment is Linu\n37: Two development directions do not provide extra measured evidence for the thesis: a count-noise/latent-target \n38: The final qualification sources record completion and a qualified outcome, with the declared checks satisfied.\n39: The complete selected procedure, not an isolated stump component, is the object supported by the final compari\n40: Search scores are not final results; the original learned ridge scale has no matched repeated-final comparator\n41: No failed final qualification is recorded; unspecified gate diagnostics remain unknown.\n42: Every supplementary utility and group/mass table entry is an arithmetic mean or sample standard deviation of t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
