rber2021.json\nBarberLimits2021\tDirect known arXiv identifier; read title, authors and abstract only; no references or related-page links accessed\tliterature_evidence/BarberLimits2021.json\nGuan2023\tDirect known arXiv identifier; read title, authors and abstract only; no references or related-page links accessed\tliterature_evidence/Guan2023.json\nVovk2012\tDirect known arXiv identifier; read title, authors and abstract only; no references or related-page links accessed\tliterature_evidence/Vovk2012.json\nBarberBeyond2023\tDirect known arXiv identifier; read title, authors and abstract only; no references or related-page links accessed\tliterature_evidence/BarberBeyond2023.json\nMeinshausen2006\tArticle landing-page abstract\tliterature_evidence/Meinshausen2006.txt\n{\n  \"claim\": \"\\\\subsection{Validity and Its Boundaries}\\nCondition on the independent samples used to fit the center and scale, including all training-only model selection. If calibration and test examples are exchangeable, their normalized scores are exchangeable under the fixed fitted functions. The rank of a new score among the calibration scores and itself gives\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n \\\\Pr\\\\{Y_{\\\\rm new}\\\\in C_\\\\alpha(X_{\\\\rm new})\\\\}\\\\ge 1-\\\\alpha.\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nTies can make coverage conservative. This is the standard split-conformal rank argument, not a new theorem \\\\citep{Vovk2005,Lei2018}. Neither a good quantile fit nor a correct noise model is required for this marginal result; those affect region allocation and efficiency. Conversely, the argument does not guarantee coverage conditional on a covariate or on a fixed subgroup. It also does not apply automatically when the test-covariate law differs from calibration. We retain those distinctions throughout the empirical interpretation.\",\n  \"source_ids\": [\n    \"ref.Lei2018\",\n    \"ref.Vovk2005\"\n  ],\n  \"citation_keys\": [\n    \"Lei2018\",\n    \"Vovk2005\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_refs\": [],\n  \"su